**Findings — spike persistence & return-rate comparison (all Region E, spike month 2025-03):**

| Combo | Spike units | Spike ret. rate | 24-mo avg ret. rate (pooled) | Pre-3-mo avg units | Post-3-mo avg units | Post/Pre | Verdict signal |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| E / Accessory | 10,421 | 5.94% | 6.13% | 6,029.7 | 6,538.3 | 1.084 | Isolated spike, but return rate **normal** (≈24-mo avg) → looks like a one-off real demand pulse (e.g., promo), not stuffing |
| E / Service | 3,809 | 0.74% | 0.78% | 2,462.3 | 2,558.3 | 1.039 | Isolated spike, return rate normal → mild real-demand bump |
| E / Hardware | 1,612 | **17.37%** | 3.92% | 1,024.3 | 1,066.3 | 1.041 | Isolated spike **and** return rate ~4.4× the 24-mo average, reverting next month → classic **channel stuffing** signature |

Key evidence:
- All three spikes are **isolated**: post-spike 3-month averages return to within ~4–9% of pre-spike levels (post/pre ≈ 1.04–1.08), so none is a sustained trend.
- **E/Hardware** is the standout stuffing candidate: units jumped 1.57× pre-level in one month, returns jumped from ~30/month to 280 (17.4% vs 3.9% baseline), then fully reverted in 2025-04 — consistent with goods pushed into channel inventory and returned.
- **E/Accessory** and **E/Service** spikes show no return-rate anomaly, so despite being isolated, they are more plausibly genuine one-off demand events than stuffing.

